[ABORTED -- killed on a wrong diagnosis, and the numbers above are not trustworthy]

Two reads of this file disagreed:

    monitor saw   16 -> 160,925  -4,370  3/4     32 -> 157,910   -7,385  4/4
    file showed   16 -> 163,130  -2,165  1/4     32 -> 150,530  -14,765  2/4

The 8-row agreed, and the monitor's 16-row repeats the 8-row's values exactly, which is what a
byte-truncated read of a partially written buffer looks like -- the monitor used `tail -c`,
which cuts at a byte boundary rather than a line boundary.  So the file was probably right and
the monitor wrong.

I did not establish that.  I inferred "two processes are interleaving into one log" from `ps`
showing two matching lines, killed the run, and only then read the command column: 3338 was the
`timeout 2400` wrapper and 3339 its python child.  One run, not two.  A nearly complete
measurement was destroyed by a diagnosis I could have checked first with the same command.

Rerun is clean, with the reader taking whole lines only.
